EXPERIMENT 1: Introduction to Various Data Visualization Tools

AIM
To study and compare commonly used data visualization tools and understand where each tool is useful.

THEORY / EXPLANATION
Data visualization presents information through charts, graphs, maps and dashboards. It helps convert raw values into a form that is easier to explore and interpret. A good visualization should communicate the main message accurately, use suitable scales and labels, and avoid unnecessary decoration.

Microsoft Excel is useful for quick charts and spreadsheet-based analysis. Python provides programmable visualization through Matplotlib, Seaborn and Plotly. R supports statistical graphics through base graphics and ggplot2. Tableau is designed for interactive visual analysis using drag-and-drop shelves and dashboards. Power BI combines data modelling, reporting and interactive business intelligence. Looker Studio supports browser-based reports and dashboards, particularly for connected data sources.

The tools differ in learning curve, customization, interactivity, data connectivity and sharing options. The best choice depends on the data, task, audience and available software.

PROCEDURE
1. Open the available tools or their official product pages.
2. Identify the main workspace and the types of data each tool can import.
3. Compare chart creation, customization, interactivity and sharing.
4. Note one suitable use case for each tool.
5. Summarize the comparison in the output sheet.

RESULT
The major visualization tools were identified and compared. Spreadsheet tools are convenient for quick summaries, Python and R provide flexible programmatic analysis, and Tableau, Power BI and Looker Studio support interactive reporting. The suitable tool depends on the analysis requirement.

PRACTICAL NOTE
This is a study-oriented experiment. Use the comparison table in Output as the practical observation. No single software is mandatory for this experiment.
